Answer:
To define a custom particle class with additional `mass` and `velocity` attributes on top of `ParticleBase`, you need to create a derived class that inherits from `ippl::ParticleBase`. In this derived class, use the `addAttribute` method provided by `ParticleBase` to add the new attributes. Here is an example:

```cpp
#include "Particle/ParticleBase.hpp"

class CustomParticle : public ippl::ParticleBase<YourLayoutType> {
public:
    using Base = ippl::ParticleBase<YourLayoutType>;

    CustomParticle() : Base() {
        // Adding mass attribute
        this->addAttribute(mass);
        mass.set_name("mass");

        // Adding velocity attribute
        this->addAttribute(velocity);
        velocity.set_name("velocity");
    }

private:
    ippl::detail::ParticleAttribBase<YourLayoutType> mass;
    ippl::detail::ParticleAttribBase<YourLayoutType> velocity;
};
```

Ensure that `YourLayoutType` is replaced with the appropriate layout type you are using in your simulation.

Evidence:
- src/Particle/ParticleBase.hpp : ParticleBase — foundational structure for particle management within IPPL.
- src/Particle/ParticleBase.hpp : addAttribute — method for adding attributes to particles.
- src/Particle/ParticleAttribBase.h : ParticleAttribBase — base class for managing particle attributes.

